      "writtenOn": "2023-04-25T14:38:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "One for \"magic_num\" defined by OEM and another for data. Thanks.",
      "parentUuid": "3f968865_3b1e17cc",
      "revId": "a4adaaab45363bd199251ee4316627280ae94482",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "19fc9c80_1310b843",
        "filename": "include/linux/pm_qos.h",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 96,
      "author": {
        "id": 1069543
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-04-25T15:16:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Is \"data\" a pointer to an OEM data struct? Could that struct contain the \"magic_num\"?",
      "parentUuid": "bacb5de3_eec57bee",
      "revId": "a4adaaab45363bd199251ee4316627280ae94482",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "78be5927_fb782255",
        "filename": "include/linux/pm_qos.h",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 96,
      "author": {
        "id": 2003165
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-04-26T11:42:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m afraid we can\u0027t. As far as I know, we need \"magic_num\" to indicate whether our data exists or not.",
      "parentUuid": "19fc9c80_1310b843",
      "revId": "a4adaaab45363bd199251ee4316627280ae94482",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f148b0bf_bc01ab31",
        "filename": "include/linux/pm_qos.h",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 96,
      "author": {
        "id": 1125653
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-04-26T12:22:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If the pointer is NULL or not should be all that you need to know. \"magic numbers\" do not work at all, please don\u0027t use them, the kernel community has been ripping the last remaining usages of them out recently.",
      "parentUuid": "78be5927_fb782255",
      "revId": "a4adaaab45363bd199251ee4316627280ae94482",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "639909ef_bfc27088",
        "filename": "include/linux/pm_qos.h",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 96,
      "author": {
        "id": 2003165
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-04-27T14:02:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Take \u0027data\u0027 as a pointer. If we only use this pointer, we can\u0027t guarantee that it is valid.",
      "parentUuid": "f148b0bf_bc01ab31",
      "revId": "a4adaaab45363bd199251ee4316627280ae94482",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "831492fb_b9e45c7a",
        "filename": "include/linux/pm_qos.h",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 96,
      "author": {
        "id": 1125653
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-04-27T14:59:11Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes you can, if it is !NULL, it is a valid pointer.  You control the setting of this field, so you can trust that you got it correct.",
      "parentUuid": "639909ef_bfc27088",
      "revId": "a4adaaab45363bd199251ee4316627280ae94482",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "944f50c1_9abb2294",
        "filename": "include/linux/pm_qos.h",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 96,
      "author": {
        "id": 2003165
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-04-28T11:24:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We do not control the setting of the `freq_qos_request` field, and we cannot take the risk of someone not following our expectations. Therefore, if it is not NULL, it may be an invalid pointer in our kernel module. Thank you.",
      "parentUuid": "831492fb_b9e45c7a",
      "revId": "a4adaaab45363bd199251ee4316627280ae94482",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a31f6be6_76148ef4",
        "filename": "include/linux/pm_qos.h",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 96,
      "author": {
        "id": 1125653
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-04-28T11:58:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "You can not trust a pointer, then you can not trust a random \"magic value\", sorry.  \n\nAnd if you can not control the setting of this, then it is not a valid OEM_DATA field to add at all, as it would be useless.",
      "parentUuid": "944f50c1_9abb2294",
      "revId": "a4adaaab45363bd199251ee4316627280ae94482",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f1b8c700_cb20626e",
        "filename": "include/linux/pm_qos.h",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 96,
      "author": {
        "id": 2003165
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-05-03T16:44:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m sorry for the confusion. The OEM_DATA field is set and used in our OEM code. However, the initialization of the freq_qos_request field may not be under our control. Therefore, if we only use one pointer, the pointer may be !NULL after the freq_qos_request field is initialized. This may make it difficult to determine whether the pointer is valid in the OEM code. Thanks.",
      "parentUuid": "a31f6be6_76148ef4",
      "revId": "a4adaaab45363bd199251ee4316627280ae94482",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    }
